IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `sfp-dates`

-- Succession Act 2006 (NSW), Chapter 3 (family provision and notional estate), from the seat of the person who wants
-- provision out of a dead person's estate.
--
-- SEAT: the applicant, or would-be applicant, for a family provision order: the spouse, de facto partner, child, former
-- spouse, dependant or close companion of the person who died. Every exported question is one that person would ask:
-- "Am I eligible?", "By when must I apply?", "Can the Court make an order for me?", "Can it reach this property?", "Can
-- this gift, joint tenancy or super benefit be brought back as notional estate?", "Is the executor protected if they
-- distribute before I claim?", "Does my release bind me?". The Court, the executor or administrator (the legal
-- representative) and the beneficiaries get no seat here: what they do is encoded as what it means for the applicant.
--
-- Provisions: Chapter 3, Parts 3.1 to 3.4 (ss 55 to 100), with the Chapter 1 definitions it uses (s 3(1), (2), (3),
-- (4)) and Schedule 1 Part 3 (deaths before Chapter 3 commenced). Out, and why: see NOTES.md.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "only if" or "unless", a flat "may not" or "must not"), or NOTHING where the Act does not
-- answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. Where the Act says the Court "may" do
-- something "if satisfied", JUST TRUE means the Court has the power on those facts; whether and how it uses it is the
-- Court's discretion, never decided here. Every judgment the Act leaves to the Court is an input, worded "the Court
-- finds ...".
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 14 July 2023; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Succession Act 2006, Chapter 3 -- family provision`

§§ `The nouns`

-- How the person asking is a "child" of the person who died. s 57(1)(c) "a child of the deceased person"; "child" is
-- not defined in the Act (a borrowed term, asked), but s 3(2) includes a child born after the death from a pregnancy
-- that began before it and who survives at least 30 days, and s 57(2) includes, where the dead person was in a de facto
-- relationship or a domestic relationship (Property (Relationships) Act 1984) at the time of death, the children of that
-- relationship listed in s 57(2)(a) to (e).
DECLARE `How I am their child` IS ONE OF
    `I am not their child`
    `I am their child`
    `I was born after they died, from a pregnancy that began before their death, and lived at least 30 days`
    `I was born of sexual relations between them and their de facto or domestic partner`
    `I was adopted by both them and their de facto or domestic partner`
    `my mother was their de facto partner and they are, or are presumed to be, my father`
    `they and their female de facto partner, both women, are presumed to be my parents`
    `they and their de facto or domestic partner both had parental responsibility for my long-term welfare`

-- Whether the person who died was, at death, in a de facto relationship or a domestic relationship (Property
-- (Relationships) Act 1984) (s 57(2)). s 57(2)(c) and (d) need a de facto relationship; the other heads either.
DECLARE `Their relationship when they died` IS ONE OF
    `they were in no de facto or domestic relationship`
    `they were in a de facto relationship`
    `they were in a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship`

-- What the person has applied for before, in relation to the same estate (s 59(3), (4)).
DECLARE `My earlier applications for this estate` IS ONE OF
    `I have not applied before`
    `a family provision order was made for me before`
    `my earlier application was refused`

-- The property the person asks an order to reach (ss 63, 64, 73(2)).
DECLARE `The property I am asking about` IS ONE OF
    `property in the estate that has not been distributed`
    `estate property the legal representative holds on trust that is not yet vested in interest in anyone`
    `estate property the legal representative distributed in compliance with section 93`
    `estate property that has been distributed in some other way`
    `property that was never part of the estate`

-- What the person who died did, or did not do (ss 75, 76). The first is the ordinary lifetime gift or transfer; the
-- next five are the s 76(2) examples; the contract is s 76(2)(f); a will is s 75(3).
DECLARE `What they did or did not do` IS ONE OF
    `they gave away or transferred property during their life`
    `they did not sever a joint tenancy before they died`
    `they did not exercise a power to appoint or dispose of property outside their estate`
    `they did not exercise a power to extinguish another person's interest in their property`
    `they did not change the beneficiary of, or surrender or deal with, a life assurance policy on their life`
    `property passed to someone because of their membership of a superannuation or other fund, scheme or body`
    `they entered into a contract disposing of property out of their estate`
    `they made a will, or did not make one`
    `some other act or omission of theirs`

-- How property came to be held outside the estate, for a notional estate order under s 79 or s 80.
DECLARE `How the property left the estate` IS ONE OF
    `it was distributed from their estate`
    `it went through a transaction they entered into`

-- How property passed on after the first recipient (ss 81, 82).
DECLARE `How the property passed on` IS ONE OF
    `the first recipient passed it on through a transaction of their own`
    `the first recipient has died and their legal representative holds it`
    `the first recipient has died and it was distributed from their estate`

-- s 88: the state of the estate.
DECLARE `The state of the estate` IS ONE OF
    `they left no estate`
    `the estate is not enough for the order the Court thinks should be made`
    `provision should not come wholly from the estate, because of other applicants or special circumstances`
    `the estate is enough`

-- What the applicant has told the legal representative (ss 93(1)(e), (3), 94(4), (5), (6)).
DECLARE `What I have told the legal representative` IS ONE OF
    `I have given the legal representative no written notice`
    `I gave written signed notice of my application or intended application`
    `I gave written notice that my application has been commenced in the Court, or served a copy of it`

-- Where costs would be paid from (ss 78(2), 99(1)).
DECLARE `Where the costs would be paid from` IS ONE OF
    `the estate`
    `the notional estate`

-- s 72(1): how an order takes effect.
DECLARE `How the order takes effect` IS ONE OF
    `as if the provision were made in a codicil to their will`
    `as if the provision were made in a will of theirs`
    `as the Court orders`

-- The party and act of the regulative rule (s 58(2)).
DECLARE `Family provision party` IS ONE OF `the applicant`
DECLARE `Family provision act` IS ONE OF `file the application in the Court's registry`

§§ `ss 3, 57 -- who is an eligible person`

-- s 3(3) "a close personal relationship is a close personal relationship (other than a marriage or a de facto
-- relationship) between two adult persons, whether or not related by family, who are living together, one or each of
-- whom provides the other with domestic support and personal care." s 3(4): it "is taken not to exist" where the care is
-- given "(a) for fee or reward, or (b) on behalf of another person or an organisation". A "means"-style definition with a
-- flat exclusion: when it fails, JUST FALSE (here, plain FALSE inside the eligibility test).
GIVEN `we were married or in a de facto relationship` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we were both adults` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we were living together` IS A BOOLEAN
      `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the care was given for fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `we had a close personal relationship` `we were married or in a de facto relationship` `we were both adults` `we were living together` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IF
        (NOT `we were married or in a de facto relationship`)
    AND `we were both adults`
    AND `we were living together`
    AND `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care`
    AND (NOT `the care was given for fee or reward`)
    AND (NOT `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation`)

-- s 57(1)(c) with s 3(2) and s 57(2). The s 57(2) heads count only "if the deceased person was in a de facto
-- relationship, or a domestic relationship ... at the time of death" (an "if": when it fails, that route is no), and
-- (c) and (d) only "in the case of a de facto relationship" (between a man and a woman; between 2 women, which the
-- option labels carry), so a domestic relationship that is not a de facto relationship does not meet them.
GIVEN `how I am their child` IS A `How I am their child`
      `their relationship when they died` IS A `Their relationship when they died`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I count as their child` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died` IF
    CONSIDER `how I am their child`
    WHEN `I am not their child` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `I am their child` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `I was born after they died, from a pregnancy that began before their death, and lived at least 30 days` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `my mother was their de facto partner and they are, or are presumed to be, my father` THEN `their relationship when they died` EQUALS `they were in a de facto relationship`  -- s 57(2)(c)
    WHEN `they and their female de facto partner, both women, are presumed to be my parents` THEN `their relationship when they died` EQUALS `they were in a de facto relationship`  -- s 57(2)(d)
    OTHERWISE NOT (`their relationship when they died` EQUALS `they were in no de facto or domestic relationship`)  -- s 57(2)(a), (b), (e)

-- s 57(1)(a), (b), (c): the heads that need no further finding under s 59(1)(b).
GIVEN `I was married to them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `how I am their child` IS A `How I am their child`
      `their relationship when they died` IS A `Their relationship when they died`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am eligible under paragraph (a), (b) or (c)` `I was married to them when they died` `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died` IF
       `I was married to them when they died`
    OR `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died`
    OR `I count as their child` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died`

-- s 57(1)(d), (e), (f). (e) "a person (i) who was, at any particular time, wholly or partly dependent on the deceased
-- person, and (ii) who is a grandchild of the deceased person or was, at that particular time or at any other time, a
-- member of the household of which the deceased person was a member". (f) "a person with whom the deceased person was
-- living in a close personal relationship at the time of the deceased person's death".
GIVEN `I was formerly married to them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am their grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we were married or in a de facto relationship` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we were both adults` IS A BOOLEAN
      `we were living together when they died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the care was given for fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am eligible under paragraph (d), (e) or (f)` `I was formerly married to them` `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` `I am their grandchild` `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` `we were married or in a de facto relationship` `we were both adults` `we were living together when they died` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IF
       `I was formerly married to them`
    OR (    `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them`
        AND (`I am their grandchild` OR `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of`))
    OR `we had a close personal relationship` `we were married or in a de facto relationship` `we were both adults` `we were living together when they died` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation`

-- s 3(1) "eligible person means a person who may make an application for a family provision order under section 57":
-- a definition the Act hands to the s 57 list, so when no head holds the answer is JUST FALSE (ruling of 2026-11-05,
-- delegated definitions (a)). Schedule 1 Part 3 cl 11(1): Chapter 3, as inserted by the 2008 amending Act, "applies in
-- relation to the estate of a person who dies on or after the commencement of this clause"; for an earlier death the
-- Family Provision Act 1982 continues to apply (cl 11(2)): outside these sources, NOTHING. The commencement day is not
-- in the deposited text; it is asked.
@export Am I an eligible person who may apply to the Court for a family provision order out of the estate of the person who died (ss 3(2)-(4), 57, Sch 1 Pt 3)?
GIVEN `they died on or after Chapter 3 commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died on or after the day Chapter 3 (inserted by the Succession Amendment (Family Provision) Act 2008) commenced, which was 1 March 2009 (that day is not in the text encoded here; if the person died earlier, the Family Provision Act 1982 applies instead)
      `I was married to them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were their spouse (married to them) at the time they died
      `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them in a de facto relationship (as defined in section 21C of the Interpretation Act 1987) at the time they died
      `how I am their child` IS A `How I am their child` @desc Whether, and how, you are their child. One of: "I am not their child", "I am their child", "I was born after they died, from a pregnancy that began before their death, and lived at least 30 days", "I was born of sexual relations between them and their de facto or domestic partner", "I was adopted by both them and their de facto or domestic partner", "my mother was their de facto partner and they are, or are presumed to be, my father", "they and their female de facto partner, both women, are presumed to be my parents", "they and their de facto or domestic partner both had parental responsibility for my long-term welfare"
      `their relationship when they died` IS A `Their relationship when they died` @desc Whether, when they died, the person was in a de facto relationship, or a domestic relationship within the Property (Relationships) Act 1984 that was not a de facto relationship (this matters only to the last five child options; the two options naming a de facto partner need a de facto relationship). One of: "they were in no de facto or domestic relationship", "they were in a de facto relationship", "they were in a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship"
      `I was formerly married to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their former spouse (you were married to them, and the marriage ended before they died)
      `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at any time you were wholly or partly dependent on them (for example financially)
      `I am their grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their grandchild
      `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the time you were dependent on them or at any other time, you were a member of a household of which they were also a member
      `we were both adults` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were both adults
      `we were living together when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were living together when they died
      `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one or each of you gave the other domestic support and personal care
      `the care was given for fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that domestic support and personal care was given for a fee or reward (for example as a paid carer)
      `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation (for example a government agency, a company or a charity)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I an eligible person` MEANS
    IF (NOT `they died on or after Chapter 3 commenced`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: the Family Provision Act 1982 applies, Sch 1 Pt 3 cl 11(2))
    ELSE IF    `I am eligible under paragraph (a), (b) or (c)` `I was married to them when they died` `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died`
            OR `I am eligible under paragraph (d), (e) or (f)` `I was formerly married to them` `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` `I am their grandchild` `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` `we are married or de facto` `we were both adults` `we were living together when they died` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 3(1) hands the definition to the s 57 list
    WHERE
        `we are married or de facto` MEANS `I was married to them when they died` OR `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died`

-- s 59(1)(b): "in the case of a person who is an eligible person by reason only of paragraph (d), (e) or (f) ... having
-- regard to all the circumstances of the case (whether past or present) there are factors which warrant the making of
-- the application". JUST TRUE: that extra finding is needed; JUST FALSE: it is not (you are eligible under (a), (b) or
-- (c)); NOTHING where you are not an eligible person at all, so the question does not arise.
@export Must the Court also find factors that warrant my application, because I am eligible only as a former spouse, a dependant or a close personal companion (s 59(1)(b))?
GIVEN `I was married to them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were their spouse (married to them) at the time they died
      `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them in a de facto relationship at the time they died
      `how I am their child` IS A `How I am their child` @desc Whether, and how, you are their child. One of: "I am not their child", "I am their child", "I was born after they died, from a pregnancy that began before their death, and lived at least 30 days", "I was born of sexual relations between them and their de facto or domestic partner", "I was adopted by both them and their de facto or domestic partner", "my mother was their de facto partner and they are, or are presumed to be, my father", "they and their female de facto partner, both women, are presumed to be my parents", "they and their de facto or domestic partner both had parental responsibility for my long-term welfare"
      `their relationship when they died` IS A `Their relationship when they died` @desc Whether, when they died, the person was in a de facto relationship, or a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship (this matters only to the last five child options; the two options naming a de facto partner need a de facto relationship). One of: "they were in no de facto or domestic relationship", "they were in a de facto relationship", "they were in a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship"
      `I was formerly married to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their former spouse
      `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at any time you were wholly or partly dependent on them
      `I am their grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their grandchild
      `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at some time you were a member of a household of which they were also a member
      `we were both adults` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were both adults
      `we were living together when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were living together when they died
      `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one or each of you gave the other domestic support and personal care
      `the care was given for fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that care was given for a fee or reward
      `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Court find factors warranting my application` MEANS
    IF `I am eligible under paragraph (a), (b) or (c)` `I was married to them when they died` `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `I am eligible under paragraph (d), (e) or (f)` `I was formerly married to them` `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` `I am their grandchild` `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` FALSE `we were both adults` `we were living together when they died` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the question does not arise: not an eligible person)

§§ `s 58 -- by when to apply`

-- s 58(2) "An application for a family provision order must be made not later than 12 months after the date of the death
-- of the deceased person, unless the Court otherwise orders on sufficient cause being shown or the parties to the
-- proceedings consent to the application being made out of time." s 58(3) "An application is taken to be made on the day
-- it is filed in the Court's registry." s 58(1): it may be made whether or not administration has been granted. s 97:
-- where the date of death is uncertain the Court may fix one. Time 0 is the day of death, in days.
GIVEN `the days in the 12 months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Family provision party` `Family provision act`
`s 58(2) -- the applicant must file within 12 months after the death` `the days in the 12 months` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `file the application in the Court's registry`
    WITHIN `the days in the 12 months`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "the application was not filed in the Court's registry within 12 months after the date of the death (s 58(2), (3))"

@export What is the last day on which I can file my application for a family provision order without needing the Court's order or the parties' consent (s 58(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (if the date is uncertain, the Court may fix one under section 97)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply` MEANS `the last day of the 12 months after` `the day they died`

-- Whether the application is made within the 12 months, run as the s 58(2) rule.
GIVEN `the day they died` IS A DATE, `the day I file` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I filed within the 12 months` `the day they died` `the day I file` IF
    `the day they died` `falls on or before` `the day I file`
    AND
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 58(2) -- the applicant must file within 12 months after the death` `the days in the 12 months`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the applicant` `file the application in the Court's registry` `the day of filing`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days in the 12 months` MEANS `the number of days from` `the day they died` `until` (`the last day of the 12 months after` `the day they died`)
        `the day of filing` MEANS `the number of days from` `the day they died` `until` `the day I file`

-- "must be made not later than ... unless": outside the 12 months with neither the Court's order nor the parties'
-- consent, JUST FALSE.
@export Can my application for a family provision order be made on the day I file it: is it in time, or has the Court or the parties allowed it out of time (s 58(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) the application in the Court's registry
      `the Court has ordered otherwise on sufficient cause being shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that you may apply out of time, on your showing sufficient cause
      `the parties consent to my applying out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties to the proceedings consent to your application being made out of time
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my application be made when I file it` MEANS
    IF    `I filed within the 12 months` `the day they died` `the day I file`
       OR `the Court has ordered otherwise on sufficient cause being shown`
       OR `the parties consent to my applying out of time`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 59 -- when the Court may make an order`

-- s 59(1) "The Court may ... make a family provision order ... if the Court is satisfied that (a) the person ... is an
-- eligible person, and (b) [for (d), (e), (f) only] ... there are factors which warrant the making of the application,
-- and (c) at the time when the Court is considering the application, adequate provision for the proper maintenance,
-- education or advancement in life of the person ... has not been made by the will ... or by the operation of the
-- intestacy rules ... or both." s 59(3): a second order "only if" (a) a substantial detrimental change, or (b) the
-- undisclosed property conditions (i)-(iii). s 59(4): after a refusal, "only if, at the time of refusal, there existed
-- all the circumstances regarding undisclosed property described in subsection (3)(b)". s 95(1): a release "has effect
-- only if it has been approved by the Court and to the extent that the approval has not been revoked". s 58(2): the time
-- limit. A failed "if", "only if" or "unless": JUST FALSE. JUST TRUE: the Court has the power; the amount is its
-- discretion (s 59(2)) and is not computed. Sch 1 Pt 3 cl 11(1), (2): a death before Chapter 3 commenced is under the
-- Family Provision Act 1982 (NOTHING). cl 11(4) extends s 59(3)(b) and (4) (undisclosed property) to an order made before
-- commencement; that lifts the bar on a further application, but whether the Court may then order provision for a
-- pre-commencement death still turns on the 1982 Act (eligibility, time, adequacy, cl 11(2), (3)), outside these
-- sources, so the answer for such a death stays NOTHING.
@export Can the Court make a family provision order for me out of the estate (ss 57, 58(2), 59, 95(1))?
GIVEN `they died on or after Chapter 3 commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died on or after 1 March 2009, when Chapter 3 commenced (that day is not in the text encoded here)
      `I was married to them when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were their spouse (married to them) at the time they died
      `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with them in a de facto relationship at the time they died
      `how I am their child` IS A `How I am their child` @desc Whether, and how, you are their child. One of: "I am not their child", "I am their child", "I was born after they died, from a pregnancy that began before their death, and lived at least 30 days", "I was born of sexual relations between them and their de facto or domestic partner", "I was adopted by both them and their de facto or domestic partner", "my mother was their de facto partner and they are, or are presumed to be, my father", "they and their female de facto partner, both women, are presumed to be my parents", "they and their de facto or domestic partner both had parental responsibility for my long-term welfare"
      `their relationship when they died` IS A `Their relationship when they died` @desc Whether, when they died, the person was in a de facto relationship, or a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship (this matters only to the last five child options; the two options naming a de facto partner need a de facto relationship). One of: "they were in no de facto or domestic relationship", "they were in a de facto relationship", "they were in a domestic relationship that was not a de facto relationship"
      `I was formerly married to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their former spouse
      `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at any time you were wholly or partly dependent on them
      `I am their grandchild` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are their grandchild
      `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether at some time you were a member of a household of which they were also a member
      `we were both adults` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were both adults
      `we were living together when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and they were living together when they died
      `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether one or each of you gave the other domestic support and personal care
      `the care was given for fee or reward` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that care was given for a fee or reward
      `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation
      `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) the application in the Court's registry
      `the Court has ordered otherwise on sufficient cause being shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that you may apply out of time, on your showing sufficient cause
      `the parties consent to my applying out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties consent to your applying out of time
      `I made a release of my right to apply that the Court approved and has not revoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you released (gave up) your right to apply for a family provision order out of the whole estate, the Court approved the release under section 95, and the approval has not been revoked
      `the Court finds factors that warrant my application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds, having regard to all the circumstances past and present, that there are factors which warrant your application (needed only if you are eligible only as a former spouse, a dependant or a close personal companion)
      `the Court finds that adequate provision has not been made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds that, at the time it considers the application, adequate provision for your proper maintenance, education or advancement in life has not been made by the will, the intestacy rules, or both
      `my earlier applications` IS A `My earlier applications for this estate` @desc What you applied for before in relation to the same estate. One of: "I have not applied before", "a family provision order was made for me before", "my earlier application was refused"
      `the Court finds a substantial detrimental change in my circumstances since the last order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds there has been a substantial detrimental change in your circumstances since a family provision order was last made for you
      `the evidence then did not reveal certain property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the earlier order was made or the earlier application refused, the evidence about the estate (including possible notional estate) did not reveal the existence of certain property
      `the Court would have found the estate substantially greater` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court would have considered the estate (including possible notional estate) substantially greater in value had the evidence revealed that property
      `the Court would not have made the earlier order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court would not have made the earlier order (or, after a refusal, would not have refused) had the evidence revealed that property
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court make a family provision order for me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`they died on or after Chapter 3 commenced`)) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: the Family Provision Act 1982, Sch 1 Pt 3 cl 11(2), with cl 11(4))
           IF (NOT `eligible under (a), (b) or (c)`) AND (NOT `eligible under (d), (e) or (f)`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 59(1)(a)
           IF (NOT (`Can my application be made when I file it` `the day they died` `the day I file` `the Court has ordered otherwise on sufficient cause being shown` `the parties consent to my applying out of time` EQUALS JUST TRUE)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 58(2) "unless"
           IF `I made a release of my right to apply that the Court approved and has not revoked` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 95(1), fork F6
           IF (NOT `eligible under (a), (b) or (c)`) AND (NOT `the Court finds factors that warrant my application`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 59(1)(b)
           IF (NOT (`the Court finds that adequate provision has not been made for me`)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 59(1)(c)
           IF `the second application test fails` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 59(3), (4) "only if"
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `eligible under (a), (b) or (c)` MEANS `I am eligible under paragraph (a), (b) or (c)` `I was married to them when they died` `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died` `how I am their child` `their relationship when they died`
        `eligible under (d), (e) or (f)` MEANS `I am eligible under paragraph (d), (e) or (f)` `I was formerly married to them` `I was at some time wholly or partly dependent on them` `I am their grandchild` `I was at some time a member of a household they were a member of` (`I was married to them when they died` OR `I was living with them in a de facto relationship when they died`) `we were both adults` `we were living together when they died` `one or each of us gave the other domestic support and personal care` `the care was given for fee or reward` `the care was given on behalf of another person or an organisation`
        `the undisclosed property circumstances` MEANS `the evidence then did not reveal certain property` AND `the Court would have found the estate substantially greater` AND `the Court would not have made the earlier order`
        `the second application test fails` MEANS
            CONSIDER `my earlier applications`
            WHEN `I have not applied before` THEN FALSE
            WHEN `a family provision order was made for me before` THEN NOT (`the Court finds a substantial detrimental change in my circumstances since the last order` OR `the undisclosed property circumstances`)
            WHEN `my earlier application was refused` THEN (NOT `the undisclosed property circumstances`)

§§ `s 98 -- mediation first`

-- s 98(2) "Unless the Court, for special reasons, otherwise orders, it must refer an application for a family provision
-- order for mediation before it considers the application." s 98(3): a consent order in the terms of a written agreement
-- produced after mediation or on a lawyer's advice (comment only: a power of the Court on the parties' agreement).
@export Must my application for a family provision order go to mediation before the Court considers it (s 98(2))?
GIVEN `the Court has ordered otherwise for special reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has, for special reasons, ordered that your application need not be referred for mediation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my application go to mediation first` MEANS
    IF `the Court has ordered otherwise for special reasons` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 63, 64, 73 -- what property an order can reach`

-- s 63(1) an order "may be made in relation to the estate"; (2) a will estate includes what would vest in the executor
-- or administrator with the will annexed; (3) "may not be made in relation to property of the estate that has been
-- distributed by the legal representative of the estate in compliance with the requirements of section 93, except as
-- provided by subsection (5)"; (4) estate property held on trust is "not having been distributed unless it is vested in
-- interest"; (5) "may be made in relation to property that is not part of the estate ..., or that has been distributed,
-- if it is designated as notional estate". s 64: property outside New South Wales "only if the deceased person was, at
-- the time of death, domiciled in New South Wales". s 73(2): Part 3.2 applies to notional estate as to estate.
@export Can a family provision order for me be made out of this property (ss 63, 64, 73(2))?
GIVEN `the property` IS A `The property I am asking about` @desc The property you are asking about. One of: "property in the estate that has not been distributed", "estate property the legal representative holds on trust that is not yet vested in interest in anyone", "estate property the legal representative distributed in compliance with section 93" (after the 6-month wait and a notice of intended distribution, with no notice of your claim), "estate property that has been distributed in some other way", "property that was never part of the estate" (for example jointly owned property that passed by survivorship, or a superannuation death benefit)
      `the Court has designated it as notional estate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has designated this property as notional estate of the person who died, by a notional estate order
      `the property is outside New South Wales` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the property is situated outside New South Wales
      `they were domiciled in New South Wales when they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was domiciled in New South Wales at the time of death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can an order for me be made out of this property` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the property is outside New South Wales` AND (NOT `they were domiciled in New South Wales when they died`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 64 "only if"
           IF `the Court has designated it as notional estate` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 63(5), 73(2)
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER `the property`
               WHEN `property in the estate that has not been distributed` THEN JUST TRUE
               WHEN `estate property the legal representative holds on trust that is not yet vested in interest in anyone` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 63(4)
               WHEN `estate property the legal representative distributed in compliance with section 93` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 63(3) "may not"
               WHEN `estate property that has been distributed in some other way` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent: s 63(3) bars only a s 93 distribution; fork F7)
               WHEN `property that was never part of the estate` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 63(5) "if" designated

§§ `ss 74-77 -- relevant property transactions`

-- s 75(1) "A person enters into a relevant property transaction if the person does, directly or indirectly, or does not
-- do, any act that (immediately or at some later time) results in property being (a) held by another person (whether or
-- not as trustee), or (b) subject to a trust, and full valuable consideration is not given to the person for doing or not
-- doing the act." s 75(3): making or not making a will "does not constitute an act or omission", except as a failure to
-- exercise a power of appointment over property outside the estate (the s 76(2)(a) option). s 76(2)(a)-(f): the
-- listed circumstances, "subject to full valuable consideration not being given". s 76(4): keeping the right of
-- survivorship is not consideration for not severing a joint tenancy. An "if" condition: when it fails, JUST FALSE.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do`
      `full valuable consideration was given to them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`it is a relevant property transaction` `what they did` `full valuable consideration was given to them` `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what they did`
    WHEN `they made a will, or did not make one` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 75(3) "does not constitute"
    OTHERWISE
        IF `full valuable consideration was given to them`
        THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 75(1), 76(1)
        ELSE
            CONSIDER `what they did`
            WHEN `some other act or omission of theirs` THEN
                IF `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
            OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- s 76(2): the gift itself and each listed circumstance put property in another's hands

@export Is what the person who died did (or did not do) with this property a "relevant property transaction" that can bring it back as notional estate (ss 74-76)?
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do` @desc What the person who died did or did not do. One of: "they gave away or transferred property during their life", "they did not sever a joint tenancy before they died" (so the property passed to the other joint owner by survivorship), "they did not exercise a power to appoint or dispose of property outside their estate", "they did not exercise a power to extinguish another person's interest in their property", "they did not change the beneficiary of, or surrender or deal with, a life assurance policy on their life", "property passed to someone because of their membership of a superannuation or other fund, scheme or body", "they entered into a contract disposing of property out of their estate", "they made a will, or did not make one", "some other act or omission of theirs"
      `full valuable consideration was given to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they received full value in return (full valuable consideration). Keeping the right of survivorship is not consideration for not severing a joint tenancy (section 76(4))
      `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, as a result, property became held by another person (as owner or trustee) or subject to a trust, at once or later (asked only for "some other act or omission of theirs")
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this a relevant property transaction` MEANS
    `it is a relevant property transaction` `what they did` `full valuable consideration was given to them` `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust`

-- s 77: when the transaction takes effect. (2): the s 76(2)(a), (c), (d) circumstances are "taken to have been entered
-- into immediately before, and to take effect on, the person's death or the occurrence of the other event resulting in
-- the person no longer being entitled to exercise the relevant power"; (3): the s 76(2)(b), (e) circumstances, on "the
-- person's death or the occurrence of the other event referred to in those paragraphs". So they take effect on the
-- death only where it was the death that ended the power, the right to sever or the membership; where another event
-- ended it, they take effect on that event's day, which is asked as the day it took effect. (1), (4): otherwise when the
-- property becomes held by another person or subject to a trust, or, for a contract with some but not full
-- consideration, when the contract is entered into; that day is asked.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a section 76(2)(a) to (e) circumstance` `what they did` IF
    CONSIDER `what they did`
    WHEN `they did not sever a joint tenancy before they died` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `they did not exercise a power to appoint or dispose of property outside their estate` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `they did not exercise a power to extinguish another person's interest in their property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `they did not change the beneficiary of, or surrender or deal with, a life assurance policy on their life` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `property passed to someone because of their membership of a superannuation or other fund, scheme or body` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do`
      `an event other than their death ended it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it takes effect on the death` `what they did` `an event other than their death ended it` IF
        `it is a section 76(2)(a) to (e) circumstance` `what they did`
    AND (NOT `an event other than their death ended it`)

-- s 80(2): the transactions s 80 applies to. (a) took effect within 3 years before the death and was entered into
-- intending, wholly or partly, to deny or limit provision; (b) took effect within one year before the death and was
-- entered into when the dead person's moral obligation to provide for an eligible applicant was substantially greater
-- than any moral obligation to enter into it; (c) took effect, or is to take effect, on or after the death. s 80(1)
-- needs the transaction to be "a transaction to which this section applies": an "if", so outside (a)-(c), no (fork F9
-- for the counting).
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do`
      `an event other than their death ended it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it took effect` IS A DATE
      `the day they died` IS A DATE
      `the Court finds it was meant to deny or limit provision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court finds their moral obligation to provide was substantially greater` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 80 applies to the transaction` `what they did` `an event other than their death ended it` `the day it took effect` `the day they died` `the Court finds it was meant to deny or limit provision` `the Court finds their moral obligation to provide was substantially greater` IF
       `it takes effect on the death` `what they did` `an event other than their death ended it`
    OR `the day they died` `falls before` `the day it took effect`
    OR (`the day it took effect` `falls within` 3 `years before` `the day they died` AND `the Court finds it was meant to deny or limit provision`)
    OR (`the day it took effect` `falls within` 1 `years before` `the day they died` AND `the Court finds their moral obligation to provide was substantially greater`)

§§ `ss 78, 83, 87-90 -- the limits on every notional estate order`

-- s 78(1) a notional estate order "only" (a) for a family provision order to be made, or (b) for a costs order (the
-- costs route is in `Can my costs be paid out of the estate or notional estate`). s 88: "must not ... unless" (a) no
-- estate, (b) the estate is insufficient, or (c) provision should not come wholly from the estate. s 90: where the
-- application was made more than 12 months after the death, or the estate has already had a family provision order,
-- "must not ... unless" (a) the property was the subject of the transaction or distribution and is held as trustee only
-- and not vested in interest in any beneficiary, or (b) "other special circumstances". s 89(3): where property became
-- held by a person as trustee only, nothing else that person holds may be designated. s 87 (matters the Court must
-- consider) puts no condition on the facts; it is a comment only. s 90(1)(a) "made later than 12 months after the date of
-- the death" is worked out from the filing day with the s 58(2) date rule (fork F3); (b) "previously the subject of a
-- family provision order" is asked.
GIVEN `the day they died` IS A DATE, `the day I file` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `my application was made later than 12 months after the death` `the day they died` `the day I file` IF
    (`the last day of the 12 months after` `the day they died`) `falls before` `the day I file`

GIVEN `a family provision order is to be made for me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the state of the estate` IS A `The state of the estate`
      `the day they died` IS A DATE
      `the day I file` IS A DATE
      `the estate has already had a family provision order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the limits in ss 78, 88, 89(3) and 90 are met` `a family provision order is to be made for me` `the state of the estate` `the day they died` `the day I file` `the estate has already had a family provision order` `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee` IF
        `a family provision order is to be made for me`
    AND (NOT (`the state of the estate` EQUALS `the estate is enough`))
    AND (NOT `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee`)
    AND (   (NOT (   `my application was made later than 12 months after the death` `the day they died` `the day I file`
                  OR `the estate has already had a family provision order`))  -- s 90(1)
         OR `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary`
         OR `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order`)

§§ `ss 79, 80 -- notional estate from a distribution or the dead person's own transaction`

-- s 79: the Court may designate property "if the Court is satisfied that on, or as a result of, a distribution of the
-- deceased person's estate, property (whether or not the subject of the distribution) became held by a person ... or
-- subject to a trust". s 80(1): "if the Court is satisfied that the deceased person entered into a relevant property
-- transaction before his or her death and that the transaction is a transaction to which this section applies". s 83:
-- for s 80 (and ss 81, 82) the Court "must not, merely because a relevant property transaction has been entered into,
-- make an order ... unless" the transaction or holding disadvantaged the estate or an eligible applicant, or involved the
-- exercise, or omission to exercise, a right or power that could have benefited them (s 83(1)(a)-(d), one finding
-- here). Then the limits of ss 78, 88, 89(3), 90. s 80(3) (property held by, or on trust for, the person who received
-- property through the transaction, or the object of the trust, whether or not it was the property transacted) is
-- carried by "this property": the question is asked about property so held. s 75(2) (a later act over the same property
-- can be a further transaction) needs no rule: each act is asked about on its own.
@export Can the Court designate this property, which a distribution or the dead person's own transaction put outside the estate, as notional estate so that an order for me can reach it (ss 75-80, 83, 88-90)?
GIVEN `how it left` IS A `How the property left the estate` @desc How the property came to be outside the estate. One of: "it was distributed from their estate", "it went through a transaction they entered into"
      `what they did` IS A `What they did or did not do` @desc For a transaction: what the person who died did or did not do. One of: "they gave away or transferred property during their life", "they did not sever a joint tenancy before they died", "they did not exercise a power to appoint or dispose of property outside their estate", "they did not exercise a power to extinguish another person's interest in their property", "they did not change the beneficiary of, or surrender or deal with, a life assurance policy on their life", "property passed to someone because of their membership of a superannuation or other fund, scheme or body", "they entered into a contract disposing of property out of their estate", "they made a will, or did not make one", "some other act or omission of theirs"
      `full valuable consideration was given to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a transaction: whether they received full value in return (keeping the right of survivorship does not count)
      `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For "some other act or omission of theirs": whether as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust
      `an event other than their death ended it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an unsevered joint tenancy, an unexercised power, a life assurance policy or a fund membership: whether it was an event other than their death that ended their right to sever, their power or their membership (or, for a fund, made the property pass), for example a power of appointment that lapsed on a set date or a benefit paid on retirement. If so, give that event's day as the day it took effect (section 77(2), (3)); if it was their death, it takes effect on the death
      `the day it took effect` IS A DATE @desc For a gift, a contract or another act: the day the property became held by the other person or subject to the trust (for a contract with some but not full value in return, the day the contract was made). For a joint tenancy, power, policy or membership ended by an event other than their death: the day of that event. Ignored where their death ended it
      `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the Court finds it was meant to deny or limit provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the transaction was entered into intending, wholly or partly, to deny or limit provision out of the estate for someone entitled to apply
      `the Court finds their moral obligation to provide was substantially greater` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds that, when the transaction was entered into, their moral obligation to provide adequately for someone entitled to apply was substantially greater than any moral obligation to enter into the transaction
      `the Court finds it disadvantaged the estate or an applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the transaction, or the holding it produced, directly or indirectly disadvantaged the estate or a person entitled to apply, or involved using (or not using) a right, discretion or power that could have benefited them (section 83)
      `a family provision order is to be made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is to make a family provision order for you in these proceedings
      `the state of the estate` IS A `The state of the estate` @desc The state of the estate. One of: "they left no estate", "the estate is not enough for the order the Court thinks should be made", "provision should not come wholly from the estate, because of other applicants or special circumstances", "the estate is enough"
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) your application for a family provision order in the Court's registry (if it is later than 12 months after the death, section 90 limits notional estate orders)
      `the estate has already had a family provision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the estate has already been the subject of a family provision order (for you or anyone else)
      `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this property was itself the subject of the transaction or distribution, the person holding it holds it only as trustee as a result, and it is not vested in interest in any beneficiary of the trust
      `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order (needed only if your application was made more than 12 months after the death or the estate already had a family provision order)
      `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is other property of a person who received property from the transaction or distribution only as a trustee (only the property they hold as that trustee can be designated)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this property be designated as notional estate for me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`the limits in ss 78, 88, 89(3) and 90 are met` `a family provision order is to be made for me` `the state of the estate` `the day they died` `the day I file` `the estate has already had a family provision order` `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee`)) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `how it left` EQUALS `it was distributed from their estate` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 79
           IF (NOT (`it is a relevant property transaction` `what they did` `full valuable consideration was given to them` `as a result property became held by another person or subject to a trust` EQUALS JUST TRUE)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 80(1)
           IF (NOT (`section 80 applies to the transaction` `what they did` `an event other than their death ended it` `the day it took effect` `the day they died` `the Court finds it was meant to deny or limit provision` `the Court finds their moral obligation to provide was substantially greater`)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 80(2)
           IF (NOT (`the Court finds it disadvantaged the estate or an applicant`)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 83 "must not ... unless"
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 81, 82 -- notional estate passed on after the first recipient`

-- s 81(1): the Court may designate property held by a third person if (a) it has (or had, just before a deceased
-- transferee's death) power to designate property held by the transferee, (b) the transferee since entered into a
-- relevant property transaction, and (c) "there are special circumstances that warrant the making of the order". s 82(1):
-- property of a deceased transferee's estate if (a) the Court had that power just before the transferee's death, (b) the
-- power "did not arise because property became held by the deceased transferee as trustee only", and (c) for
-- distributed property (s 82(2)(b)), special circumstances; s 82(2)(a) needs administration of the transferee's estate
-- to have been granted (s 91 lets the Court grant it for the purpose). s 83 applies to both. Then ss 78, 88, 89(3), 90.
-- s 81(2) and s 82(2) (which property of the third person, or of the deceased transferee's estate or its
-- distributees, may be designated) are carried by "the property": the question is asked about property so held.
@export Can the Court designate property that the first recipient of a gift, distribution or transaction has since passed on (or left in their own estate) as notional estate for me (ss 81-83, 88-90)?
GIVEN `how it passed on` IS A `How the property passed on` @desc How the property passed on from the person who first received it from the dead person (the transferee). One of: "the first recipient passed it on through a transaction of their own", "the first recipient has died and their legal representative holds it", "the first recipient has died and it was distributed from their estate"
      `the Court has power to designate what the first recipient held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has (or, if the first recipient has died, had just before their death) power to designate property held by the first recipient as notional estate (for example because they received a gift caught by section 80)
      `the first recipient's dealing was a relevant property transaction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a recipient who passed it on: whether, since receiving it, they entered into a relevant property transaction (for example a gift without full value in return)
      `that power arose only because the first recipient held property as trustee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a recipient who has died: whether the Court's power over them arose only because they held property as a trustee
      `administration of the first recipient's estate has been granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a recipient who has died and whose legal representative holds the property: whether probate or administration of their estate has been granted
      `the Court finds special circumstances that warrant the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds special circumstances that warrant the order (needed for property passed on by a transaction, or distributed from the first recipient's estate)
      `the Court finds it disadvantaged the estate or an applicant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the transaction or the holding disadvantaged the estate or a person entitled to apply, or involved using (or not using) a right or power that could have benefited them (section 83)
      `a family provision order is to be made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is to make a family provision order for you in these proceedings
      `the state of the estate` IS A `The state of the estate` @desc The state of the estate. One of: "they left no estate", "the estate is not enough for the order the Court thinks should be made", "provision should not come wholly from the estate, because of other applicants or special circumstances", "the estate is enough"
      `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day I file` IS A DATE @desc The day you file (or filed) your application for a family provision order in the Court's registry (if it is later than 12 months after the death, section 90 limits notional estate orders)
      `the estate has already had a family provision order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the estate has already been the subject of a family provision order (for you or anyone else)
      `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this property was itself the subject of the transaction or distribution, is held only as trustee as a result, and is not vested in interest in any beneficiary
      `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order (needed only if your application was made more than 12 months after the death or the estate already had a family provision order)
      `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is other property of a person who received property only as a trustee
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can property passed on by the first recipient be designated as notional estate for me` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`the limits in ss 78, 88, 89(3) and 90 are met` `a family provision order is to be made for me` `the state of the estate` `the day they died` `the day I file` `the estate has already had a family provision order` `the holder holds it as trustee only, from the transaction or distribution, not vested in any beneficiary` `the Court finds other special circumstances that justify the order` `it is other property of someone who received property only as trustee`)) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF (NOT (`the Court has power to designate what the first recipient held`)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 81(1)(a), 82(1)(a)
           IF (NOT (`the Court finds it disadvantaged the estate or an applicant`)) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 83
           OTHERWISE
               CONSIDER `how it passed on`
               WHEN `the first recipient passed it on through a transaction of their own` THEN
                   IF `the first recipient's dealing was a relevant property transaction` AND `the Court finds special circumstances that warrant the order` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 81(1)(b), (c)
               WHEN `the first recipient has died and their legal representative holds it` THEN
                   IF (NOT `that power arose only because the first recipient held property as trustee`) AND `administration of the first recipient's estate has been granted` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 82(1)(b), (2)(a)
               WHEN `the first recipient has died and it was distributed from their estate` THEN
                   IF (NOT `that power arose only because the first recipient held property as trustee`) AND `the Court finds special circumstances that warrant the order` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 82(1)(b), (c), (2)(b)

§§ `s 89(2) -- how much can be designated`

-- s 89(2) "The Court must not designate as notional estate property that exceeds that necessary, in the Court's opinion,
-- to allow the provision that should be made, or, if the Court makes an order that costs be paid from the notional estate
-- under section 99, to allow costs to be paid as ordered, or both." Read arithmetically (fork F10): the provision the
-- Court thinks should be made, plus the costs ordered from the notional estate, less what the actual estate can supply
-- towards them; never below nil.
@export What is the most, in value, that the Court can designate as notional estate for my order and costs (s 89(2))?
GIVEN `the provision the Court thinks should be made for me` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the provision the Court is of the opinion should be made for you, in dollars
      `the costs ordered to be paid from the notional estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs the Court orders to be paid from the notional estate under section 99, in dollars (0 if none)
      `what the estate itself can supply towards them` IS A NUMBER @desc The value the actual estate can supply towards that provision and those costs, in dollars (0 if there is no estate)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most that can be designated as notional estate` MEANS
    max 0 ((`the provision the Court thinks should be made for me` PLUS `the costs ordered to be paid from the notional estate`) MINUS `what the estate itself can supply towards them`)

§§ `ss 93, 94 -- if the legal representative distributes before my claim`

-- s 93(1): the legal representative "may distribute" if (a) at least 6 months after the death, (b) notice of intended
-- distribution in the approved form has been given, (c) the time it specifies is not less than 30 days after the notice,
-- (d) that time has expired, and (e) at the time of distribution the legal representative has no notice of any
-- application or intended application. s 93(2): then not liable to an applicant of whom they had no notice, if the
-- distribution was in accordance with the section and properly made. s 93(3), 94(6): notice must be in writing, signed
-- as the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 require. s 94(1): not liable for a proper distribution for the immediate
-- maintenance or education of a wholly or substantially dependent eligible person, notice or no (94(2)). s 94(3): no
-- action by a person of full capacity who notified in writing their consent, or that they would not apply. s 94(4): a
-- representative with notice of an intended application is not liable for a distribution made "in compliance with
-- section 93 (1)" not earlier than 12 months after the death; (5) unless they had written notice that the application
-- was commenced, or were served. Read (fork F12) as compliance with s 93(1)(a)-(d), since (e) can never hold for a
-- representative who has notice. A protection whose "if" fails: JUST FALSE ("these sections give no protection on
-- these facts", never "the representative is liable"), as with a defence (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)).
@export If the executor or administrator distributes the estate before my claim is dealt with, are they protected from liability to me (ss 93, 94)?
GIVEN `the day they died` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `the day of the distribution` IS A DATE @desc The day the legal representative (the executor or administrator) distributed, or will distribute, the property
      `the legal representative gave notice of intended distribution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the legal representative gave notice, in the approved form, that they intend to distribute the estate after a specified time
      `the day that notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day that notice of intended distribution was given
      `the day the notice specified` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice specified, after which the estate would be distributed
      `what I have told the legal representative` IS A `What I have told the legal representative` @desc What you had told the legal representative, in writing signed as court documents must be, before the distribution. One of: "I have given the legal representative no written notice", "I gave written signed notice of my application or intended application", "I gave written notice that my application has been commenced in the Court, or served a copy of it"
      `the distribution was properly made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was properly made (for example to the right people, in the right shares)
      `I consented in writing or said in writing I would not apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, while of full legal capacity, notified the legal representative in writing that you consent to the distribution, or that you do not intend to make an application that would affect it
      `it was for the immediate needs of a dependent eligible person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the distribution was to provide what was immediately necessary for the maintenance or education of an eligible person who was wholly or substantially dependent on the person who died just before their death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the legal representative protected if they distribute before my claim` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it was for the immediate needs of a dependent eligible person` AND `the distribution was properly made` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 94(1), (2)
           IF `I consented in writing or said in writing I would not apply` AND `the distribution was properly made` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 94(3)
           IF `section 93(1)(a) to (d) are met` AND `what I have told the legal representative` EQUALS `I have given the legal representative no written notice` AND `the distribution was properly made` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 93(1)(e), (2)
           IF `section 93(1)(a) to (d) are met` AND `what I have told the legal representative` EQUALS `I gave written signed notice of my application or intended application` AND `the 12 months have passed` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 94(4), (5)
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- no protection under ss 93, 94 on these facts
    WHERE
        `section 93(1)(a) to (d) are met` MEANS
                (`the day they died` `moved by months` 6) `falls on or before` `the day of the distribution`
            AND `the legal representative gave notice of intended distribution`
            AND (`the number of days from` `the day that notice was given` `until` `the day the notice specified`) AT LEAST 30
            AND `the day the notice specified` `falls before` `the day of the distribution`
        `the 12 months have passed` MEANS (`the last day of the 12 months after` `the day they died`) `falls on or before` `the day of the distribution`

§§ `ss 95, 96 -- releasing the right to apply`

-- s 95(1) "A release by a person of the person's rights to apply for a family provision order has effect only if it has
-- been approved by the Court and to the extent that the approval has not been revoked by the Court." (2): approval may be
-- sought before or after the death. (4): the Court weighs advantage, prudence, fairness and independent advice (its
-- judgment, folded into "approved"). With no release made, the question does not arise: NOTHING.
@export Does my release of my right to apply for a family provision order have effect (s 95(1))?
GIVEN `I made a release of my right to apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed a release of your right to apply for a family provision order, or agreed to sign one
      `the Court approved the release` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court approved the release under section 95
      `the Court has revoked its approval` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has since revoked its approval (under section 96)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my release of my right to apply have effect` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`I made a release of my right to apply`)) THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (the question does not arise)
           IF `the Court approved the release` AND (NOT `the Court has revoked its approval`) THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- "only if"

-- s 96(1) "The Court may not revoke an approval of a release ... except as provided by this section." (2) if satisfied
-- that its approval was obtained by fraud, or the release by fraud or undue influence; (3) if all persons sufficiently
-- affected consent.
@export Can the Court revoke its approval of my release of my right to apply (s 96)?
GIVEN `the Court finds its approval was obtained by fraud` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that its approval of the release was obtained by fraud
      `the Court finds the release was obtained by fraud or undue influence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied that the release itself was obtained by fraud or undue influence
      `everyone sufficiently affected consents to revocation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all persons who would, in the Court's opinion, be sufficiently affected by the revocation consent to it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the approval of my release be revoked` MEANS
    IF    `the Court finds its approval was obtained by fraud`
       OR `the Court finds the release was obtained by fraud or undue influence`
       OR `everyone sufficiently affected consents to revocation`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 70 -- an order already made for someone else`

-- s 70(2): the Court may vary or revoke an order "so as to allow provision to be made in favour of another eligible
-- person"; (3) "must not ... unless that person shows sufficient cause for not having applied ... before the order sought
-- to be varied or revoked was made". The question assumes you are an eligible person (ask that first); fork F13.
-- s 70(1) (an order may be varied or revoked "only in accordance with this Chapter") is machinery, a comment only.
-- s 70(4) (an order is revoked if the grant of administration is revoked or rescinded, unless the Court otherwise
-- provides) is left out: it turns on the Court's revocation of the grant under the Probate and Administration Act 1898,
-- not deposited, and on the Court's choice to provide otherwise.
@export If a family provision order has already been made for someone else, can it be varied or revoked so that provision is made for me (s 70(2), (3))?
GIVEN `I show sufficient cause for not applying before that order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you show sufficient cause (a good reason) for not having applied before the existing order was made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can an existing order be varied to provide for me` MEANS
    IF `I show sufficient cause for not applying before that order` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `ss 78(2), 99 -- costs`

-- s 99(1) "The Court may order that the costs of proceedings under this Chapter ... (including costs in connection with
-- mediation) be paid out of the estate or notional estate, or both". s 78(2) "The Court must not make an order under
-- subsection (1)(b) for the purposes of an order that the whole or part of an applicant's costs be paid from the
-- notional estate ... unless the Court makes or has made a family provision order in favour of the applicant." Maximum
-- costs under s 99(2) regulations are not deposited.
@export Can the Court order that my costs of the proceedings be paid out of the estate, or out of notional estate (ss 78(2), 99(1))?
GIVEN `where from` IS A `Where the costs would be paid from` @desc Where the costs would be paid from. One of: "the estate", "the notional estate"
      `a family provision order is made or has been made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court makes, or has made, a family provision order in your favour
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my costs be paid out of the estate or notional estate` MEANS
    CONSIDER `where from`
    WHEN `the estate` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the notional estate` THEN
        IF `a family provision order is made or has been made for me` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 78(2)

§§ `s 72 -- how an order takes effect`

-- s 72(1) "A family provision order takes effect, unless the Court otherwise orders, as if the provision was made (a) in
-- a codicil to the will of the deceased person, if the deceased person made a will, or (b) in a will of the deceased
-- person, if the deceased person died intestate."
@export How does a family provision order made for me take effect (s 72(1))?
GIVEN `they left a will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died made a will (if not, they died intestate)
      `the Court has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that the provision takes effect in some other way
GIVETH A `How the order takes effect`
`How does an order for me take effect` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Court has ordered otherwise` THEN `as the Court orders`
           IF `they left a will` THEN `as if the provision were made in a codicil to their will`
           OTHERWISE `as if the provision were made in a will of theirs`
